From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:27:58 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0301): peer identity check with trust on first use
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A peer is its RustDesk ID plus the fingerprint of its Ed25519 signing key
(DEF-3, PLAN §7). Before the host's Hash is read, and so before any
password-derived data leaves, the controller compares the identity the
stream proved with the pin saved in the peer's config:

- first use: show ID and fingerprint and pin only after the user
  confirms (AC-43). The stream that waited on the dialog is then dropped
  unused (the host may have timed it out meanwhile) and a fresh
  connection logs in, passing only if it proves the key just pinned; the
  credential is saved by the normal login path on it, so it too exists
  only after confirmation. The answer is bound to that prompt's id and
  the host ID and fingerprint it showed (a concurrent prompt's answer
  confirms nothing), and the pin is committed compare-and-set. No
  exemptions, switch-sides included;
- key or ID mismatch: stop without logging in, with a Forget peer path
  (AC-37, AC-43);
- no identity proved (stock host, keyless server, plaintext fallback):
  stop.

ID routes keep upstream's hbbs-signed key. Direct routes (IP connect)
had no identity and no encryption at all: the host's direct listener
now runs the identity handshake, and SignedId carries the host's
signing key (new field remotesmith_identity_pk = 1001) so the controller
can verify the signature and pin the key. The host's own fingerprint is
upstream's (Settings > About, ipc get_config("fingerprint")).

Consequence: a stock RustDesk client can no longer connect by IP to a
RemoteSmith host (it never answers the handshake); both ends run
RemoteSmith.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
